      {
        "id": 234106,
        "name": "der andere Jan",
        "username": "der_andere_Jan",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/der_andere_jan/{size}/1881_2.png",
        "created_at": "2020-10-30T16:29:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>If you are worried - in practical use, this turns out much less OP than it looks.</p>\n<p>Firstly, it is easily countered by a lot of small-damage instances (basically the opposite of the ‘armored’ trait).</p>\n<p>And then, if used excessively, any GM will be quick to come up with a twist that mitigates that combo at the first opportunity.</p>\n<p>However, <span class=\"bbcode-i\">getting</span>&nbsp;this combo is not so easy in the first place, at least if you demand a somewhat convincing backstory from your players - in character creation / narrative mechanics, those two abilities are about as far apart from each other as can be. So we’re somehow back to the “Mr Superamazing” example&nbsp;mentioned in the creation foreword. You technically <span class=\"bbcode-i\">can</span> go there, but you’ve been warned about shoehorning yourself some ‘ideal’ Hero.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>EDIT:</p>\n<p>Forgot to mention, both abilities are available in RED, so the fight is a mere few moves away from being decided. That is the right moment for a superawesome last stand thing, so why not! But it is far too late&nbsp;into the scene to set the course for much of the action.</p>\n<p>And if you want to use that combo&nbsp;in YELLOW, that’s two minor twists per use in my book, so should rarely be worth it for a little damage avoidance.</p>",
        "post_number": 6,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:10:06.593Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
